{
  "version": "3.3.22",
  "source": "Check ids from the Checkov source at tag 3.3.22 (codeload.github.com/bridgecrewio/checkov/tar.gz/refs/tags/3.3.22, sha256 52d5bfee7b4047ac3eb2711568505751045c8ebef2ef41333acc86c103e7b7da), every framework Warden runs with checkov -d. Left out: secrets, sca_package and sca_image (skipped by the sandbox), and the github, gitlab and bitbucket VCS-configuration checks (CKV_GITHUB_*, CKV_GITLAB_*, CKV_BITBUCKET_*), which only run against a VCS API. Severities are Warden's, assigned by the rubric below from each check's name and code; OSS Checkov ships none.",
  "rubric": {
    "critical": "Anyone on the internet can read or change data, or get admin access, without a credential: public buckets, containers, snapshots, registries, queues, topics and resource policies (Principal \"*\", allUsers, all authenticated users); SSH, RDP or all ports open to 0.0.0.0/0; anonymous or AlwaysAllow cluster APIs; a CI OIDC trust any repository can assume. Also credentials hardcoded in IaC (provider keys, user or custom data, Lambda environment, Parameter Store) and a reverse shell in a pipeline.",
    "high": "Data at rest or in transit with no encryption at all (plain HTTP, FTP, telnet, TLS off); privilege escalation and container breakout (privileged, host namespaces, root user, docker socket, SYS_ADMIN, Tiller, RBAC that escalates, binds, impersonates or reads all secrets); overly broad IAM (wildcard actions, resources or principals, admin or basic roles, credentials exposure, write-all CI tokens); authentication switched off (no API authorizer, Lambda URL auth NONE, Redis AUTH off, no MFA); a database, cache, broker or cluster API with a public address or a firewall open to any IP; a missing account-level public-access guardrail (S3 Block Public Access, blob anonymous access, GCS public access prevention); whether what is installed or run can be trusted (package signature or TLS verification off, CI shell injection, secrets sent with curl).",
    "medium": "Logging, audit, monitoring, alerting and threat-detection gaps. Hardening: encryption with the provider's key rather than a customer-managed one, TLS version floors and cipher policies, public network access on PaaS services whose firewall still denies by default, public IPs on compute, private endpoints and VNets, WAF, key and secret rotation and expiry, password policy, local, shared-key or password authentication that still needs a credential, default service accounts and namespaces, admission controllers, network policies, automatic security patching. A Checkov check missing from this table is medium.",
    "low": "Best practice and hygiene: backups, point-in-time recovery, versioning, deletion protection, multi-AZ and zone redundancy, replication, SLA tiers, health checks and probes, resource requests and limits, managed identities, image tag and digest pinning, signing and SBOM evidence, Dockerfile style.",
    "info": "Labels, tags, descriptions, naming and other documentation.",
    "ties": "A check that fits two levels takes the one that says what an attacker gains; the severity rates the misconfiguration, not the resource. Ids shared by several frameworks (Terraform, CloudFormation, ARM, Bicep, CDK, Kubernetes and Helm) get one severity."
  },
